title: The Harbour
start: start

== start ==
bg river_jetty
music room_tone
The pilot boat is eighteen feet of fibreglass with a wheel, a radio, and a bench
that is wet whatever you do. You have taken it out four thousand times and you
have never once been out in it for pleasure.
The job is to meet ships at the bar and bring them in, because a ship's master
does not know this river and you do, and the river is not the same river twice.
There is a book in the wheelhouse. Every job goes in it: the ship, the draft,
the tide, the time you went alongside and the time you came off. Eleven years
of it.
-> the_call

== the_call ==
At 03:40 the radio brings the night voice, which is a person you have never met
and have spoken to four hundred times.
voice: Pilot, this is the harbour. The Kestrel is at the bar, inbound.
you: Draft?
voice: Six point one.
You know the river at six point one on a falling tide. You know it the way you
know your own stairs in the dark, and you know that six point one on this tide
is not a job, it is a calculation with a margin of about forty minutes.
you: What is she carrying?
voice: Timber.
* Ask what the weather is doing out there. -> ask_weather
* Say you will take her. -> take_her

== ask_weather ==
voice: Force seven, west, building. She has been hove to since two.
you: And they want to come in tonight.
voice: They want to come in tonight.
You look at the glass, which is dropping, and you do the thing you have done
four thousand times, which is arithmetic that has somebody else's ship at one
end of it.
-> the_decision

== take_her ==
you: I'll take her.
voice: She's at the fairway buoy.
You put the radio down and you put your coat on, and you do not do any
arithmetic at all, because you have done it before and you know what it says.
-> the_decision

== the_decision ==
The margin is forty minutes. The weather is building. Timber on a six-point-one
draft in a force seven on a falling tide is a calculation you have run in your
head in the dark more times than you can count, and the answer is usually yes,
because the answer is usually yes.
But it is not always yes. Twice in eleven years it has been no, and both times
you said no on the radio and both times the ship stood off and both times you
wrote it in the book and both times nobody thanked you and nobody had to.
* Take her in. -> take_her_in
* Refuse. -> refuse

== take_her_in ==
You go out. It takes eleven minutes to the fairway and the sea outside the bar
is doing what the glass said it would.
The Kestrel is a grey ship with a green funnel and she is rolling like something
that would rather not be here, and you come alongside and you climb a ladder
that has been wet since before you were born, and you stand on her bridge and
you say the first thing you always say.
you: I have her.
master: She's yours.
You bring her in. It takes an hour and ten minutes and you do not think about
the margin once, because thinking about it while you are inside it is a way of
losing it.
~ brought_her_in true
-> the_book

== refuse ==
you: Harbour, Pilot. I'm not taking her tonight.
voice: Say again?
you: Six point one on a falling tide with force seven building. Tell her master
to stand off until the tide. I'll take her at eight.
There is a silence with a person in it.
voice: He won't like it.
you: He'll like it less at the bar.
~ refused true
-> the_book

== the_book ==
bg river_jetty
You write it in the book, because the book is the job. Ship, draft, tide, time
alongside, time off. And in the last column, which is not ruled and which nobody
has ever asked you about, a word.
Tonight the word is one of two things and you have not written it yet.
* "Took her." -> wrote_took
* "Stood her off." -> wrote_stood
* Write nothing in the last column. -> wrote_nothing

== wrote_took ==
You write *took her* and you close the book, and in the morning the harbour
office rings to ask how she came in, and you say she came in fine, which is
true, and you do not say that you had forty minutes and used all of them.
-> the_morning

== wrote_stood ==
You write *stood her off* and you close the book, and in the morning the harbour
office rings to ask why, and you say because six point one on that tide with
force seven is a job for daylight, and they say the master is complaining, and
you say he is welcome to.
-> the_morning

== wrote_nothing ==
You leave the last column empty, which you have never done, and you close the
book, and you sit in the boat with the engine off and the rain on the wheelhouse
roof, and you think about the fact that the column exists because you made it
and that the only person who has ever read it is you.
~ wrote_nothing true
-> the_morning

== the_morning ==
bg river_jetty
music fan_hum
At 07:00 the day voice comes on the radio and it is somebody young, and the
first thing they say is that the Kestrel is still at the bar and asking for a
pilot at eight.
* Take her at eight, like you said. -> took_at_eight [if refused]
* Take her at eight, and say nothing about last night. -> took_at_eight [if brought_her_in]
* Take her at eight and tell them what the column is for. -> tell_them [if wrote_nothing]
* Take her at eight and say nothing. -> took_at_eight

== tell_them ==
you: There's a column in the book nobody asked for. I've been writing in it for
eleven years. It says whether I took her or stood her off, and nothing else, and
it is the only record of the two times I said no.
There is a pause on the radio that is not a radio pause.
voice: What did you write last night?
you: Nothing. That's the first time.
voice: Then write it now. I'll wait.
-> took_at_eight

== took_at_eight ==
You take her at eight. The tide is making and the wind has dropped and it is the
most ordinary job you have done all week.
Afterwards you write it in the book: the ship, the draft, the tide, the time
alongside and the time off.
And in the last column, in your own hand, a word.
-> END
